 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q replacement, tracked privately by the worker over two queues of cached sheets:
+
+- **Probationary queue**: a first-use sheet enters here when its render completes. Entries leave in first-in, first-out order.
+- **Protected queue**: a sheet moves here when it is served from the cache while in the probationary queue. Entries are ordered by recency of use; a hit on a protected sheet makes it most recent.
+
+A sheet's queue membership changes only on a cache hit, never on a miss or on completion of a shared job. A one-pass screening therefore cycles through the probationary queue without displacing sheets that have been reused.
+
+When admission requires space, eviction removes the head of the probationary queue while that queue holds more than its target share of the 12 GiB budget; otherwise it removes the least recently used protected sheet. If the chosen queue is empty, eviction uses the other. The target share is a tunable parameter, not reserved space: the queue under its share may use bytes the other does not claim. Queue accounting is in bytes of cached JPEG data, since sheet sizes vary.
+
+The queues and the target share are private to the preview worker. The external interface, render queue concurrency, and disk budget are the same under any eviction policy.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of a small set of clips, and 2Q keeps reused comparison sheets from being evicted by one-use screening sheets. The cost is two queues and a share parameter to tune. LRU has the least bookkeeping but lets a long screening evict useful comparison sheets. Aged frequency counts retain repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can keep a past project's favorites too long. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Initial value of the probationary target share, and how it is tuned against observed screening and comparison workloads.
+2. Whether a sheet evicted from the probationary queue leaves any record, so a quick re-request can be recognized as reuse.
+3. Whether queue membership survives a worker restart or every cached sheet restarts as probationary.
